Q: Cannot read properties of undefined {reading: &#39;name&#39;} A: <p>In this line of your Commodities.js, it should be <code>category?.name</code> because the first component renders before the data is ready. That's why <code>category</code> is not defined.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;h1 id=&quot;heading&quot;&gt;{category?.name} :&lt;/h1&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Why does useEffect run multiple times even with empty dependency array A: <p>As it was mentioned in other comments, this is intentional behaviour of strict mode that is supposed to identify potential problems with React concurrent rendering. <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79870891">Trying to avoid it</a> is a bad practice.</p>
<p>If the requirement is to avoid unnecessary API calls due to increased server load or quota limits, the first call can be cancelled when an effect is repeatedly triggered in strict mode. For a general API call, it can be postponed with debounced function, e.g. Lodash debounce:</p>
<pre><code>  const debouncedApiCall = useCallback(debounce(apiCall, 0), []);

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    debouncedApiCall(query);

    return () =&gt; debouncedApiCall.cancel();
  }, [debouncedApiCall]);
</code></pre>
<p>For fetch API, a request <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79522585/3731501">can be aborted with <code>AbortController</code></a>. It's not guaranteed by specs that a request won't be made, but in existing implementations it works as intended.</p>

Q: Programming C - Debugging Numbers-Strings Conversion Problems A: <p>Reviewing the task at hand, using the Luhn algorithm for credit card number testing actually works well if the credit card number is actually treated as a string of digit characters.  However, if the exercise requires the entry of a long integer, one can still utilize standard string functionality to convert the entered value into a string and then analyze each digit character for determination of a valid card number and/or a valid card vendor.</p>
<p>There seemed to be a lot of jumping back and forth between characters and numeric values.  With that, following is a refactored example that utilizes such string functions.</p>
<pre><code>void Company_Check(char * number);
bool Luhn (char * number);

int main(void)
{
    char work[30];
    //Prompt for card Number
    long card = get_long(&quot;Number:&quot;);

    sprintf(work, &quot;%ld&quot;, card);         /* Store entered credit card number as a character array    */

    //Applying Luhn's Algorithm
    bool validity = Luhn(work);

    if (validity==true) //The card passed the test
    {
        //Finding and printing company name
        Company_Check(work);
    }
    else   //The card didn't pass the test
    {
        printf(&quot;Invalid - (Luhn’s Algorithm)\n&quot;);
    }
}

void Company_Check(char * number)
{
    //Analizing cases
    if ((number[0] == '3') &amp;&amp; ((number[1] == '4') || (number[1] == '7')))
    {
        printf(&quot;American Express\n&quot;);
    }
    else if ((number[0] == '5') &amp;&amp; ((number[1] == '1') || (number[1] == '2') || (number[1] == '3') || (number[1] == '4') || (number[1] == '5')))
    {
        printf(&quot;Mastercard\n&quot;);
    }
    else if (number[0] == '4')
    {
        printf(&quot;Visa\n&quot;);
    }
    else
    {
        printf(&quot;Invalid vendor card prefix\n&quot;);
    }
}

bool Luhn (char * number)
{
    bool odd = true;
    int work, sum = 0;

    for (int i = strlen(number) - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--)   /* Read cc number string from right to left */
    {
        if (odd)
        {
            sum += (number[i] - '0');               /* Add derived digit value to summation     */
            odd = false;
        }
        else
        {
            work = (number[i] - '0') * 2;           /* Multiply even numbered digits by two     */

            if (work &gt; 9)                           /* If value is greater than nine, then      */
                work -= 9;                          /* subtract nine from the value which       */
                                                    /* effectively adds the two digits together */
            sum += work;
            odd = true;
        }
    }

    if (sum % 10 == 0)                              /* Is resulting summation divisible by ten  */
    {
        return true;
    }
    else
    {
        return false;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>With that, following are a few results at the terminal testing both theoretically correct credit card numbers and invalid numbers.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/cc_2/bin/Release$ ./cc_2 
Number:5700000000000000
